    "answer": {
      "short": "$x_T=\\dfrac{p+q}{2}$. The tangents at $x=p$ and $x=q$ have equations $y=(2ap+b)x+c-ap^2$ and $y=(2aq+b)x+c-aq^2$; equate them.",
      "worked": "At $x=t$, the gradient is $2at+b$. Using the point-gradient formula,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(2at+b)(x-t)+(at^2+bt+c)\\\\y&=(2at+b)x+c-at^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nEquate the tangent equations for $t=p$ and $t=q$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(2ap+b)x+c-ap^2&=(2aq+b)x+c-aq^2\\\\2a(p-q)x&=a(p^2-q^2)\\\\2x&=p+q\\\\x_T&=\\frac{p+q}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe stated intersection makes the two tangents distinct, so $a(p-q)\\ne0$ when dividing.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
